---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/expert/computational-neuroscience">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the summit of the cellular-and-molecular unit, and every earlier lesson becomes
    a load-bearing wall here. You need the resting and firing machinery from <em>The neuron</em>
    and <em>The action potential</em>, the synaptic chemistry from <em>Neurons and synapses</em>,
    and the receptor pharmacology of <em>Neurotransmitters and drugs</em>. Nothing is re-taught
    from scratch; instead each piece of machinery is asked the expert question — what does it
    compute?
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why a model is a theory stated precisely enough to run, with Hodgkin–Huxley as the founding template;</li>
    <li>describe population coding and its noise-robust read-out, and contrast it with single-cell precision;</li>
    <li>treat synapses as dynamic filters — release probability, facilitation, depression — and place inhibition as a computational resource;</li>
    <li>state the formal learning rules (Hebb, spike-timing-dependent plasticity, homeostatic scaling) and connect dopamine to reward prediction error;</li>
    <li>trace how a molecular lesion propagates through network dynamics into circuit failure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational neuroscience treats the nervous system as an <strong>information-processing
    system whose algorithms can be stated precisely and tested</strong>. It asks what problems
    the brain solves (vision, motor control, prediction), what representations it uses (rates,
    spikes, populations), and what circuits compute them — the meeting point of neuroscience,
    mathematics and the AI ladder of this atlas. Its method is the model: write the theory
    exactly enough to run, then compare against the biology. If the model reproduces the
    phenomenon, the theory survives; if not, the theory was vaguer than it felt. Because this
    lesson sits at the summit of a unit on cellular and molecular neurobiology, it works through
    that substrate in order — each section takes one piece of neural machinery and asks how
    computation enters it, from the resting potential to the genes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the modelling stack, from encoding to efficiency" widget="equationStep" config={compneuroCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> press "Next step" and treat each card as a promise this
    lesson will keep. Step 1 is the method — run the theory, let the biology grade it; step 2
    defines representation as the encoding–decoding pair; step 3 stakes the boldest claim,
    that task pressure alone can push artificial networks toward brain-like solutions; step 4
    is the constraint that disciplines all the rest, the ~20-watt budget. Watch how each card
    converts a descriptive statement into a testable one.
  </p>

  <h2>Neural networks as the frame</h2>
  <p>
    The unit's opening topic — neurons and neural networks — becomes a computational claim:
    nervous tissue is a network of leaky, nonlinear integrators, and network behaviour explains
    things single cells cannot. A neuron sums thousands of inputs (roughly 10,000 synapses per
    cortical neuron; about 86 billion neurons in a human brain, Herculano-Houzel's count) with
    different time constants and weights — an astonishingly literal cousin of the artificial
    perceptron, with the biological version adding recurrent loops and continuous adaptation. The
    computational payoff is population coding: represent a quantity (a direction, a face, a
    location) across many noisy neurons rather than one precise one. Georgia Georgopoulos's
    motor-cortex work showed a monkey's reaching direction can be read out as a weighted vector
    across hundreds of neurons, each broadly tuned — robust to noise in a way a single-cell code
    could never be.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="which curve matches the population code?" widget="trace" config={cnPopTrace} />

  <h2>Resting and action potentials: the Hodgkin–Huxley model</h2>
  <p>
    The resting potential (about −70 mV, set by potassium leak and the Na⁺/K⁺ pump) and the
    action potential it makes possible received the field's founding model. Hodgkin and Huxley
    (1952, Nobel 1963) fitted four coupled differential equations to squid giant axon data —
    conductances for sodium (about 120 mS/cm²), potassium (36) and leak (0.3) against their
    equilibrium potentials (+50, −77, −54 mV) — and reproduced not just the spike but its
    threshold, refractory period and propagation speed. This is the computational epistemology in
    miniature: a model precise enough to be <em>wrong</em> in specific, informative ways. It also
    settled an old debate — the spike is an all-or-none regenerative event, not a decayed
    summons — and set the template every later model (integrate-and-fire, multicompartment)
    still simplifies or extends.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each HH constant with its value" widget="match" config={cnHhMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "action potentials are like current flowing down a wire." No
    charge flows end to end; each patch of membrane regenerates the signal from stored ionic
    gradients, which is why propagation is actively refreshed at every node — and why demyelination
    (as in multiple sclerosis) slows or blocks a signal a passive wire would carry fine.
  </p>

  <h2>Ionic mechanisms: why the brain runs on batteries</h2>
  <p>
    The ionic machinery is itself a computational substrate. Concentration gradients (sodium
    high outside, potassium high inside) are metabolic batteries, charged by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase
    that consumes most of the brain's ~20 watts. Computationally this buys a variable the
    artificial world lacks: excitability is state-dependent. Ion channel densities, local
    extracellular potassium and neuromodulation all tune how far a given input sits from
    threshold, so the same synapse has different gain on different days. Modellers capture this
    with the cable equation and its two constants: the membrane time constant (a few tens of
    milliseconds in many neurons — the cell's integration window) and the length constant (a
    fraction of a millimetre of dendrite — how far a signal rides before decaying). Dendritic
    trees are therefore not passive wiring but spatially distributed filters — a design the
    engineering ladder rediscovers in analogue computing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the faulty line — a modeller's dendrite notes" widget="spot-error" config={cnDendriteSpot} />

  <h2>Propagation: speed as a design constraint</h2>
  <p>
    Propagation speed is where anatomy meets algorithm. Unmyelinated axons conduct at about
    0.5–2 m/s (slow C pain fibres run at the bottom of that range); myelinated fibres saltate
    between nodes of Ranvier at up to ~120 m/s. The engineering trade-off is explicit: myelin
    buys speed at the cost of space and metabolic maintenance, so evolution spends it where
    latency matters — motor commands, proprioception — and saves it where it does not. The
    computational lesson generalises: nervous systems are real-time control systems, and every
    representation must respect its own latency budget. Reflex arcs are the minimal case (spinal
    processing before cortical awareness); long-range forebrain loops are the expensive case, and
    their delay is measurable in behaviour.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="place the marker — the slow end of the latency budget" widget="place" config={cnSpeedPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the membrane machinery — pumps, gradients, cable constants,
    myelin — sets speed and latency budgets that every neural algorithm must respect. Next,
    the quantal release experiment shows how the brain's own randomness became one of
    neuroscience's most precise measurements.
  </p>

  <h2>The neuromuscular junction: the quantal release experiment</h2>
  <p>
    The skeletal neuromuscular junction earned its computational fame as the experiment that
    revealed how release works. Bernard Katz and colleagues recorded tiny spontaneous
    depolarisations — miniature end-plate potentials of roughly 0.5 mV — and showed the evoked
    response comes in integer multiples of this unit: transmitter is released in <em>quanta</em>,
    one vesicle at a time. A nerve impulse triggers the near-synchronous release of a hundred or
    more quanta, depolarising the muscle fibre well past threshold — a built-in safety factor
    that fails in myasthenia gravis, where autoantibodies eat receptors and the safety margin
    collapses into fatigability. The quantal analysis — comparing the statistics of miniature
    and evoked potentials — is one of neuroscience's great pieces of inference from noise, and
    the probabilistic release model it produced still drives simulator design.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the magnitude — the quantal unit" widget="estimate" config={cnMiniEstimate} />

  <h2>Neurotransmitter release: SNAREs and the release code</h2>
  <p>
    Mechanistically, release is a molecular machine with a timing budget. Calcium enters through
    voltage-gated channels clustered at the active zone; sensors like synaptotagmin bind it;
    SNARE proteins zip the vesicle into the membrane; fusion takes about a millisecond; the
    vesicle is retrieved and refilled within seconds. The 2013 Nobel (Südhof, Schekman, Rothman)
    mapped this machinery. Computationally, the important quantity is the release probability
    (a synapse's Pr): short-term facilitation and depression are changes in Pr across spikes,
    meaning synapses are dynamic filters that perform temporal computation — emphasising bursts,
    adapting to rates, detecting coincidences — before the postsynaptic cell computes anything
    at all.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="rebuild the release millisecond" widget="order" config={cnReleaseOrder} />

  <h2>CNS synaptic transmission: excitation, inhibition, balance</h2>
  <p>
    Central synapses add the computational dance the reflex arc lacks: massive excitation
    (glutamate, at roughly 80–90% of cortical synapses) counterweighted by inhibition (GABA),
    held in careful balance. The electrical geometry explains why: cortical neurons receive far
    more excitatory than inhibitory contacts, so synchrony and stability require inhibition to
    be powerful, fast and precisely timed — lose it and circuits run away into epilepsy. Models
    of balanced networks (Van Vreeswijk & Sompolinsky) showed why cortical firing is irregular
    yet stable: inhibition tracks excitation moment to moment. Excitatory synapses also carry
    plasticity machinery — the NMDA receptor, a coincidence detector gated by voltage and
    transmitter — which is the hook on which learning hangs.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "inhibition just brakes the brain down." Fast inhibition is a
    computational resource — it sharpens timing windows, sculpts receptive fields (lateral
    inhibition in retina creates edge detection) and implements gain control. A cortex without
    precise inhibition could not represent anything reliably.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="your circuit model keeps running away — decide" widget="branch" config={cnInhibitBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> excitation and inhibition are balanced partners, and fast
    inhibition turns out to be a computational resource rather than a brake. The stage is now
    set for the write mechanism — the rules that change those synapses in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Plasticity: the learning rules</h2>
  <p>
    Synaptic plasticity is the brain's write mechanism, and it has formal rules. Bliss and Lømo
    (1973) found long-term potentiation in hippocampus: brief high-frequency stimulation
    strengthens synapses for hours to years. Hebb's 1949 conjecture — cells that fire together
    wire together — acquired a biophysical substrate in the NMDA receptor's coincidence
    detection. Spike-timing-dependent plasticity sharpened the rule: pre-before-post within
    tens of milliseconds potentiates; the reverse order depresses. Homeostatic mechanisms
    (synaptic scaling) renormalise total drive so the network does not silently saturate —
    a two-layer architecture of local learning rules plus slow global bookkeeping that machine
    learning would independently reinvent.
  </p>

  <h2>Cell types: computation by diversity</h2>
  <p>
    The brain is not a soup of identical units; it is a partitioned computer. Ramón y Cajal's
    neuron doctrine and the modern census distinguish pyramidal cells (excitatory output),
    interneurons in dozens of molecular flavours (fast-spiking parvalbumin basket cells setting
    precise timing windows, somatostatin cells gating dendrites, VIP cells disinhibiting others)
    and glia doing metabolic and ionic housekeeping. Circuit models show cell-type diversity is
    not decoration: connect excitatory and inhibitory classes with the observed statistics and
    stable, irregular, synchronisable dynamics fall out; connect homogeneous units and they do
    not. Cell type is where microcircuit function is encoded — and where single-cell
    transcriptomics is now redrawing the parts list at scale.
  </p>

  <h2>Synapse formation, survival and elimination: pruning as programming</h2>
  <p>
    Development is computational too: the genome cannot specify 10¹⁴ connections, so it
    specifies an overproduction rule plus an activity-based selection rule. Synapses form in
    excess, then experience prunes them — the clearest human case is the visual cortex, where
    ocular-dominance columns refine during critical periods gated by inhibitory maturation
    (Hubel & Wiesel's kitten work; Nobel 1981). Mechanistically, complement-tagging (C1q, C3)
    flags weak synapses for microglial removal — the same immune machinery that prunes, over-
    prunes in mouse models of Alzheimer's. The computational reading: development installs a
    learning rule whose data is early experience, which is why timing (critical periods) matters
    as much as amount.
  </p>

  <h2>Transport and secretion: the logistics layer</h2>
  <p>
    A neuron is a logistics problem: proteins are made in the soma but used metres away (motor
    neurons have axons up to about a metre long). Kinesin and dynein motors walk cargo along
    microtubules at 1–3 μm/s, fast axonal transport delivering vesicles and mitochondria,
    slow transport bulk-moving cytoskeleton. Secretion adds another layer: dense-core vesicles
    carrying neuropeptides release from sites distinct from small-vesicle transmitter release,
    on slower timescales. The computational point is that cellular housekeeping constrains
    algorithms — replenishment rates set how long a busy synapse can sustain release, and
    transport defects present first as distal neuropathies. Biology's compute is physical, and
    its supply chain shows.
  </p>

  <h2>Acetylcholine: the neuromodulation lesson</h2>
  <p>
    Acetylcholine is the systems case of a modulator. At the neuromuscular junction it acts
    fast and ionotropic (nicotinic receptors); in cortex it acts slower and metabotropic
    (muscarinic), released from basal forebrain projections that fire in relation to attention
    and behavioural state. Cholinergic modulation changes the cortex's computational regime:
    it suppresses intracortical traffic and enhances feedforward sensory gain — the signature of
    attention sharpening inputs (the mechanism behind nicotine's cognitive effects, and the
    pathway lost early in Alzheimer's disease, which is why cholinesterase inhibitors were its
    first symptomatic drugs). One transmitter, two receptor families, two timescales: a
    hardware-level illustration that what a molecule "means" depends on which receptors express it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> neuromodulation is hardware-level state control — one
    transmitter, different receptor families, different computational regimes. Dopamine next
    turns modulation into the field's sharpest hypothesis test: a biological variable that a
    mathematical theory predicted in advance.
  </p>

  <h2>Biogenic amines: dopamine as the teaching signal</h2>
  <p>
    The biogenic amines (dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline) are the brain's diffuse broadcast
    system — few neurons, vast projections — and dopamine became computational neuroscience's
    most famous hypothesis test. Schultz's recordings (1990s) showed midbrain dopamine neurons
    encode reward prediction error: they burst for unexpected rewards, pause for omitted ones,
    and shift to the cue once learned. That is precisely the error signal reinforcement-learning
    theory (Rescorla–Wagner, TD learning) said a learner needs — a biological variable that
    matched a mathematical one before anyone looked. It generalises: serotonin carries state
    and cost signals, noradrenaline surprise and alertness; the modulators parameterise the
    learning problem rather than transmitting percepts. Drugs from cocaine to antipsychotics
    are, in effect, interventions on this scalar teaching system.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "dopamine is the pleasure chemical." Reward prediction error
    is not pleasure: dopamine neurons respond to cues predicting reward and to errors, and their
    optogenetic stimulation motivates work without sensory pleasure. Addiction skews learning
    about prediction, not simply enjoyment — which is why anhedonic patients can still crave.
  </p>

  <h2>Amino acid transmitters: the workhorses</h2>
  <p>
    Glutamate and GABA are the volume trade of the brain — fast, cheap, everywhere — and their
    receptors define the circuit's speed limit. AMPA receptors mediate millisecond excitation;
    NMDA receptors add voltage-gated coincidence detection and calcium entry (the plasticity
    gate); GABA-A receptors are chloride channels, GABA-B metabotropic. Computational
    consequences follow the receptors: AMPA/NMDA ratio tunes integration windows; GABA reversal
    potential sets whether inhibition is subtractive or shunting; and astrocytic glutamate
    uptake keeps the workspace clean, because extracellular glutamate is excitotoxic (the
    mechanism of stroke damage). Nearly every circuit model of cortex is, quietly, a model of
    these two transmitters in balance.
  </p>

  <h2>Neuropeptides and nitric oxide: computing without wires</h2>
  <p>
    Two exotic channels of signalling complete the picture. Neuropeptides (substance P,
    neuropeptide Y, orexin, oxytocin) release from dense-core vesicles, diffuse beyond the
    synapse, and act for seconds to minutes — volume transmission that sets behavioural states
    (hunger, stress, bonding) rather than single signals. Nitric oxide is stranger still: a
    gas synthesised on demand, it diffuses freely (ignoring membranes) and signals
    retrograde — back from postsynaptic cell to presynaptic terminal, linking NMDA activity to
    presynaptic change. Its discovery earned the 1998 Nobel. Both break the wiring diagram's
    assumptions: some computation in the brain is broadcast and wireless, which models capture
    with global modulatory fields rather than point-to-point connections.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file each signal — wired or wireless" widget="sort-buckets" config={cnWirelessBuckets} />

  <h2>Genetics and neuronal disease: from gene to circuit failure</h2>
  <p>
    The unit ends where medicine begins: inherited circuit failure. The clean cases are
    deterministic — Huntington's disease is a CAG-repeat expansion in the HTT gene; above about
    40 repeats the disease is essentially fully penetrant, and repeat length predicts age of
    onset, making genotype a quantitative predictor. Most neuropsychiatric genetics is instead
    polygenic: schizophrenia associates with hundreds of common variants (plus rare structural
    mutations such as the C9orf72 repeat in ALS and frontal-temporal dementia), each of tiny
    effect — genome-wide studies now explain a useful but partial share of liability, and
    polygenic risk scores remain probabilistic, not diagnostic. Computationally these are
    models of cascade failure: a molecular lesion (a channel, a synaptic protein, a pruning
    gene) propagates through network dynamics until behaviour changes. The molecular-to-network
    pipeline is why neuroscience needs all its levels at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: in Schultz's recordings, how did midbrain dopamine neurons respond to unexpected rewards, to expected rewards, and to rewards that failed to arrive?</li>
    <li>Application: in myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies destroy acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. Explain the resulting fatigability using the quantal safety factor.</li>
    <li>Why-reasoning: why can the genome not specify the brain's 10¹⁴ connections directly, and what rule does it use instead?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) They burst for unexpected rewards, pause when an expected
    reward is omitted, and shift their response to the predicting cue once learning is
    complete. That pattern is reward prediction error — precisely the error signal
    reinforcement-learning theory (Rescorla–Wagner, TD learning) said a learner needs, and
    explicitly not a pleasure signal. (2) Release still happens in quanta, but each quantum's
    transmitter now finds far fewer receptors to act on, so the end-plate potential no longer
    clears threshold reliably. The built-in safety factor collapses, and the junction
    fatigues as use depletes it further — which is why myasthenia presents as weakness that
    worsens through the day. (3) Ten-to-the-fourteen addresses would overflow any genome;
    instead it specifies an overproduction rule plus an activity-based selection rule, so
    early experience itself does the wiring. Development installs a learning rule, and its
    data is experience — which is why critical-period timing matters as much as total amount.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A computational model is a theory stated precisely enough to run — Hodgkin–Huxley set the template.</li>
    <li>Population coding trades single-cell precision for noise-robust read-out across many broadly tuned neurons.</li>
    <li>Synapses are dynamic filters: release probability, facilitation and depression compute before the postsynaptic cell does.</li>
    <li>Fast inhibition is a computational resource — timing windows, gain control, circuit stability.</li>
    <li>Plasticity has formal rules: Hebb's conjecture, spike-timing windows, and homeostatic scaling as slow global bookkeeping.</li>
    <li>Dopamine implements reward prediction error — biology matched a mathematical variable before anyone looked.</li>
    <li>Development wires by overproduction plus activity-based pruning; inherited disease is cascade failure through the same circuits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lesson <em>Consciousness and the hard problem</em> asks what the
    computational story leaves out — why any of this processing is accompanied by experience
    at all — and reviews the neural-correlate programmes tested against it. For the
    plasticity rules in their natural habitat, revisit the advanced lesson <em>Memory
    circuits</em>, where LTP and its consolidation machinery meet the anatomy that uses them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Encoding and decoding, formally.</strong> The field's two fundamental questions are
    quantitative: encoding (given a stimulus, what response distribution?) and decoding (given
    spikes, what stimulus estimate — and with what uncertainty?). Information theory supplies
    the measures: spike counts carry a few bits per neuron per trial; populations recover more
    by pooling correlated noise. The honest limit: correlation itself is ambiguous — correlated
    variability can help or hurt decoding depending on structure — which is why population
    recordings replaced single-neuron abstractions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Bayesian brain and predictive processing.</strong> Perception is statistically
    ambiguous, so Helmholtz's "unconscious inference" became formal: the brain combines priors
    with likelihoods near-optimally (Knill & Pouget's evidence review), explaining illusions as
    correct Bayesian answers to misleading inputs. Friston's free-energy programme generalises
    this into prediction-error minimisation across cortical hierarchies — mathematically dense,
    empirically productive, and criticised for being hard to falsify at full scope. Deep networks
    trained on natural images developing representations strikingly like visual cortex
    (Yamins & DiCarlo) is the same story from the engineering side: the task, not the
    blueprint, shapes the representation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The efficiency frontier.</strong> The brain does its ~20 watts of computation with
    spikes that are sparse, event-driven and locally plastic — roughly nine orders of magnitude
    more energy-efficient per operation than current datacentres. Neuromorphic chips (event-driven
    silicon following spike timing), sparse coding and local learning rules are the engineering
    harvest of the biology. Whether brain-style learning rules can close the gap on
    backpropagation-scale training is one of the live arguments of the 2020s — and the reason
    computational neuroscience and AI have effectively merged.
  </p>

